WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.337
An AI agent buys a seven-cent article.

00:00:03.337 --> 00:00:08.265
Paying for it through PayPal today costs forty-nine cents in fees.

00:00:08.665 --> 00:00:13.465
And a reader who wants one article is asked to buy a whole subscription.

00:00:13.465 --> 00:00:15.598
So small purchases never happen.

00:00:16.498 --> 00:00:17.992
UnitMarket fixes that.

00:00:17.992 --> 00:00:22.813
Buy just what you need, let agents pay in cents, and let PayPal settle.

00:00:23.913 --> 00:00:25.296
Here is the working app.

00:00:25.296 --> 00:00:28.350
A reader says what they need, and what they will pay.

00:00:29.013 --> 00:00:35.605
The app searches only authorised offers, and proposes a pack within budget, here from two publishers.

00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:37.546
Nothing is charged until the reader approves.

00:00:37.546 --> 00:00:42.129
The price is frozen, and each publisher gets its own PayPal payment.

00:00:41.713 --> 00:00:45.143
This take runs in dry run, on a pretend PayPal.

00:00:45.143 --> 00:00:50.908
The same code made real PayPal sandbox payments on the twenty-ninth of October.

00:00:51.313 --> 00:00:54.875
Access opens only after the payment is verified.

00:00:54.875 --> 00:00:56.582
Receipt, library, done.

00:00:59.613 --> 00:01:01.908
Now hand that power to an agent.

00:01:01.908 --> 00:01:05.279
Through our API, the buyer sets a one-time cap:

00:01:05.279 --> 00:01:07.718
five dollars, and three purchases.

00:01:07.718 --> 00:01:12.021
An operator, with a second factor, switches agent buying on.

00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:15.464
The agent buys on its own, and the cap stops it.

00:01:16.764 --> 00:01:19.377
Agents buy in five to ten cent pieces.

00:01:19.377 --> 00:01:25.084
Here are ten thousand simulated trades, from a hundred agents and forty publishers.

00:01:26.584 --> 00:01:32.943
Paid one by one, the fees come to four thousand nine hundred dollars, six times what was bought.

00:01:32.943 --> 00:01:37.977
Our ledger nets the trades into a hundred and ninety-eight PayPal movements:

00:01:37.977 --> 00:01:42.283
eighty-three dollars, fifty-nine times cheaper at today's prices.

00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:45.528
At a micro-settlement price we propose to PayPal:

00:01:45.528 --> 00:01:46.787
twenty-one dollars.

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:50.832
Pillars one and two are consolidation.

00:01:52.732 --> 00:01:55.752
Buyers who want the same unit share one payment:

00:01:55.752 --> 00:01:59.400
ten thousand payments become two hundred and thirty-three.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.867
Small publishers pool on a topic to fill bundles none could fill alone:

00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:08.711
thirty-three of thirty-three, and they earn, instead of losing money to fees.

00:02:10.111 --> 00:02:10.787
Behind it:

00:02:10.787 --> 00:02:20.863
two repositories, three hundred and thirty-five thousand lines of TypeScript, more than fourteen thousand tests, and eighty-one merged pull requests.

00:02:21.663 --> 00:02:26.911
Four roles, ten buyer personas, forty content domains, tested end to end.

00:02:27.711 --> 00:02:31.295
UnitMarket does the matching. PayPal stays the rails.

00:02:32.095 --> 00:02:33.898
Every scene is labelled:

00:02:33.898 --> 00:02:37.428
real sandbox, dry run, simulation, or proposal.

00:02:38.628 --> 00:02:42.492
Group the demand. Group the supply. Make cents worth paying.

00:02:42.492 --> 00:02:47.580
That is a ride-hailing model for payments, and we want to build it with PayPal.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.804
We built the proof.

00:02:50.004 --> 00:02:53.545
UnitMarket. PayPal settles. We make cents worth paying.
